// How to find what's slow: build with -profile time, run, read profile.txt.
//
// `mara build profiling -profile time -release` makes a program that times its
// funs. When it exits (main's end, an assert, a crash) it writes profile.txt
// next to the exe: one line per fun, the most time first. Profile the
// -release build: a debug build's times say little about the optimized one.
// After a crash, the profile's top lists the calls that were running: where
// it crashed.
//
// The columns:
//   total ms    time in the fun, the funs it calls included
//   self ms     time in its own code: where to look first
//   calls       how many times it ran
//   avg us, min us, median us, max us
//               one call's time. A max far above the median is a call that
//               was slow once: a stutter.
//   probes %    how much of total is the profiler's own cost. Each timed call
//               costs its caller a few hundredths of a microsecond; a fun
//               making millions of tiny calls is mostly that, and its
//               numbers say more about the profiler than about it.
//
// A fun that calls nothing and doesn't loop (`wrap` below) isn't timed: it's
// over too quickly for the timing to be worth its cost. Its time is its
// caller's self time.
//
// For how full your partial arrays and arenas get, `-profile memory`: see
// memory_profiling.mara.
module profiling

use mara.core

// Calls nothing, doesn't loop: not timed.
wrap :: fun(x: i64) -> i64 {
	return (x * 31 + 7) % 1000003
}

// Loops: timed. Its self time has wrap's in it.
step :: fun(seed: i64) -> i64 {
	t := seed
	for i in 0..<10000 { t = wrap(t + i) }
	return t
}

main :: fun() {
	s := 0
	for frame in 0..<100 { s += step(frame) }
	print(s)
}
